1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:07,223
You lent a friend 5 million won;
1 million came back.

2
00:00:07,290 --> 00:00:10,113
Hiring help can cost more
than what's left,

3
00:00:10,180 --> 00:00:14,993
and your records are scattered:
chats, receipts, an IOU.

4
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:17,970
ClaimBack My Money helps you
organize them yourself.

5
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:22,743
Upload what you already have:
two chat screenshots,

6
00:00:22,810 --> 00:00:25,553
two transfer receipts and the IOU.

7
00:00:25,620 --> 00:00:29,650
Each file gets a SHA-256 fingerprint
on arrival.

8
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:34,013
A rule-based coordinator runs
the role agents,

9
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:36,813
and three Meta models
do the AI work.

10
00:00:36,880 --> 00:00:41,630
Muse Spark 1.2 reads each image
and returns text with its position;

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:45,463
tall screenshots are read
in overlapping tiles.

12
00:00:45,530 --> 00:00:50,993
SAM 3.1 finds visual regions:
chat bubbles, profile pictures,

13
00:00:51,060 --> 00:00:53,473
the signature box, the red seal.

14
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:57,233
Then Muse Spark 1.3 organizes the facts.

15
00:00:57,300 --> 00:00:59,900
Calls and costs are counted live.

16
00:01:00,550 --> 00:01:04,030
Nothing goes into a document
until you confirm it.

17
00:01:04,150 --> 00:01:07,300
Each value links back to the spot
it was read from:

18
00:01:08,550 --> 00:01:12,100
click a source, and that box
on the evidence flashes.

19
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:17,470
You check the principal, the dates
and the repayment against the original.

20
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:23,610
The IOU spells the borrower Alex J. Kim;
the chats say Alex Kim.

21
00:01:23,720 --> 00:01:26,583
The tool doesn't guess. It asks.

22
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:30,610
You choose the spelling, and you pick
which statements to highlight.

23
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:36,673
Account, ID and phone numbers are masked
in the image pixels

24
00:01:36,740 --> 00:01:41,723
before anything is packaged,
not hidden under a PDF layer.

25
00:01:41,790 --> 00:01:46,950
A pixel check confirms each mask;
anything it cannot confirm is flagged.

26
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:51,933
Confirm, and the calculation is done
by code, not AI:

27
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,183
the repayment goes to late-payment
interest first, then principal,

28
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:00,450
and what's left is computed
down to the won.

29
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:05,853
Next, Muse Spark 1.3
drafts the fact sentences,

30
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:07,853
each citing its evidence.

31
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:13,303
It never writes a number or a name,
only slots,

32
00:02:13,370 --> 00:02:15,393
filled from the facts you confirmed.

33
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:18,133
Even a number typed by hand is caught:

34
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:22,230
re-check, and gate G1a
blocks the sentence.

35
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:25,530
Put the slot back, and approve.

36
00:02:26,980 --> 00:02:31,260
Then your Korean drafts are ready
to review, send and file yourself:

37
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:36,513
a certified demand letter,
an exhibit bundle with redacted copies,

38
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:40,913
an evidence list, and the payment-order
text to paste into Korean e-Court.

39
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:45,250
Every PDF is checked again
against the facts you confirmed.

40
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:47,533
ClaimBack My Money.

41
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:51,543
You confirm every fact;
code does the math.

42
00:02:51,610 --> 00:02:54,593
Built during this hackathon
with Meta's models.

43
00:02:54,660 --> 00:02:58,220
Self-help draft. Not legal advice.
